      "level": "5-10",
      "priority": "high",
      "tags": [
        "architecture",
        "project",
        "rag",
        "azure"
      ],
      "why": "Whether you can draw the whole system with the real components, and explain why each piece is where it is. **Also asked as:** “Walk me through its architecture.” · “What architecture did you use?”",
      "quick": [
        "Two paths: background ingestion and a live question path.",
        "Ingestion: Blob Storage, Celery workers, parsers, embeddings, Elasticsearch.",
        "Questions: FastAPI on AKS running a LangGraph flow.",
        "Hybrid search, bge-reranker, then GPT-4o over a private endpoint.",
        "Langfuse, Prometheus and Grafana watch every request."
      ],
      "simple": "The system has two paths that share one index. The **ingestion path** runs in the background. PDFs land in Azure Blob Storage, and each new file starts a Celery job, with Redis as the queue. A worker parses the file (PyMuPDF, pdfplumber, Camelot, PaddleOCR), saves the parsed JSON back to Blob, chunks it by heading, masks sensitive data with Presidio, embeds the chunks with text-embedding-3-small on Azure OpenAI, and writes them to Elasticsearch on AKS with their metadata.\n\nThe **question path** is live. The web UI calls a FastAPI service on AKS, which runs 3 to 10 pods behind an NGINX ingress. Each request checks the user's Azure AD groups, then runs a LangGraph flow: GPT-4o-mini rewrites the question if it's a follow-up, Elasticsearch runs BM25 and kNN together with filters, a self-hosted bge-reranker-base picks 5 of 30 candidates, the flow grades whether they answer the question, and GPT-4o writes the answer with citations. Tokens stream back over SSE. Chat sessions live in Redis and LangGraph checkpoints in PostgreSQL, so any pod can serve any user.\n\nAround both paths sit Langfuse traces for every request, Prometheus and Grafana for latency, errors and token cost, and Azure DevOps pipelines that run the golden-set evaluation before each deploy. The design choice worth defending is separation: a slow ingestion job can never slow down a user's question.",
