      "say": "My default is structure-aware chunking: split by headings into 500 to 800 token sections, with about 10% overlap when a long section is cut. Every table is its own Markdown chunk with its headers, and parent-child retrieval means the LLM gets the whole section. But no, one strategy isn't enough, even among PDFs. SOPs are numbered procedures, so I keep a procedure whole with its warnings, because a step separated from \"isolate first\" is a safety problem. Datasheets are mostly tables. Inspection reports are short findings, one chunk each. Scanned pages lose font sizes after OCR, so headings come from numbering like 4.2.1, with recursive splitting as a fallback. So it's one pipeline with a router in front, and every route writes the same chunk format and metadata. We started with a single fixed-size splitter, and replacing it took context recall from about 0.70 to 0.85. The cost is upkeep: each new document type needs its own rule and test.",
      "numbers": "Sections 500-800 tokens, about 10% overlap, tables about 30% of chunks, about 15% of documents scanned. Context recall about 0.70 to 0.85 after the change.",
      "wrong": "\"We used RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter with 1,000 characters for everything.\" It's fine for a prototype, but it cuts tables and procedures. Our own first fixed-size version cost about 15 points of context recall.",
      "follow": "How do you know the scanned-page fallback is working?",
      "followAnswer": "I slice the golden set by source, so questions answered from scanned documents get their own hit@5 and recall numbers instead of hiding in the average. I also sample a few dozen scanned chunks each release and check the cuts by eye. When that slice lagged, the fix was better heading detection from numbering patterns, not a different chunk size."
