      "simple": "I compared rerankers on the 300-question golden set, measuring hit@5 and latency on our own hardware.\n\n- **bge-reranker-base** (about 280 million parameters): our production choice. It lifted hit@5 from about 0.79 to 0.88 and adds about 150 ms for 30 candidates.\n- **bge-reranker-large**: about 1 point better on hit@5, at roughly twice the latency and memory. Not worth it for us.\n- **ms-marco MiniLM cross-encoder**: very fast, but trained on web search questions, so it was noticeably weaker on equipment specs and tables.\n- **An LLM as reranker** (GPT-4o-mini scoring chunks): decent quality, but around a second and a per-call cost, so no.\n- **Hosted rerank APIs** such as Cohere Rerank: good quality, but they would send document text outside our network, which our security rules didn't allow.\n\nReranking cost grows with the number of pairs and their length, so that's where the controls are. We rerank only 30 candidates. Each pair is capped at the model's 512-token limit, which our chunk sizes fit. All 30 pairs go in **one batched call**, not 30 calls. The model runs as its own service on a small GPU node in half precision, loaded once and kept warm, so there's no start-up delay. And a **timeout** of about 1 second falls back to the RRF order, so a slow reranker makes an answer slightly worse, never late.\n\nThe limit: the 150 ms depends on that GPU. On CPU the same model was several times slower, which is the main hosting cost of reranking.",
